1
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:08,230
This video shows you how to send your records as logs, over OpenTelemetry, to a Collector.

2
00:00:08,280 --> 00:00:11,568
Your records already go to Elasticsearch and to S3.

3
00:00:11,568 --> 00:00:15,372
Many teams gather their logs in an OpenTelemetry Collector,

4
00:00:15,372 --> 00:00:18,402
which sends them on to whatever tools they use.

5
00:00:18,402 --> 00:00:22,190
Send your records there too, and they join that stream.

6
00:00:22,240 --> 00:00:24,899
The Collector asks for a bearer token.

7
00:00:24,899 --> 00:00:31,166
Keep the whole header value in a secret: the word Bearer, a space, and the token.

8
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:41,452
A log record has fields of its own, so give this destination its own pipeline.

9
00:00:41,452 --> 00:00:43,952
On the tutorial route, add a pipeline.

10
00:00:43,952 --> 00:00:47,590
It becomes a route of its own, from the same source.

11
00:00:47,640 --> 00:00:49,203
It asks for a capture.

12
00:00:49,203 --> 00:00:54,778
Start at the earliest record, stop once records come in, and use them.

13
00:01:02,480 --> 00:01:07,040
smeltr reads them as JSON, and proposes a parser step.

14
00:01:08,840 --> 00:01:10,118
Then an Eval step.

15
00:01:10,118 --> 00:01:15,089
The message becomes the body, every record gets a severity and a time,

16
00:01:15,089 --> 00:01:19,523
and the service goes in the resource, as service.name.

17
00:01:35,640 --> 00:01:41,129
Name it tutorial-otel, and save it on a branch called deliver-otel.

18
00:01:47,080 --> 00:01:51,079
From the new pipeline, add a destination.

19
00:01:51,079 --> 00:01:52,167
OTLP.

20
00:01:55,280 --> 00:01:56,650
Call it otel-logs.

21
00:01:56,650 --> 00:02:02,830
Give it the Collector's address, without a path: smeltr adds the one for logs.

22
00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:07,338
Add a credential for the Authorization header, from your secret.

23
00:02:08,440 --> 00:02:14,350
Under Advanced, paste the authority that signed the Collector's certificate.

24
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:16,031
Add it, and save.

25
00:02:20,760 --> 00:02:22,064
Open it for review.

26
00:02:22,064 --> 00:02:25,496
Three things are added: the destination otel-logs,

27
00:02:25,496 --> 00:02:33,390
a route that sends tutorial-in through the pipeline tutorial-otel to that destination, and the pipeline itself.

28
00:02:33,440 --> 00:02:35,957
Approve and deploy it.

29
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:48,078
Send a fresh record.

30
00:02:59,400 --> 00:03:03,973
The Collector prints it as a log record: the service in its resource,

31
00:03:03,973 --> 00:03:07,290
a severity, and your message as the body.

32
00:03:09,680 --> 00:03:11,506
Check on learning.

33
00:03:11,706 --> 00:03:16,718
Why add a pipeline, instead of changing tutorial-in-parse?

34
00:03:21,118 --> 00:03:24,952
tutorial-in-parse also feeds your other destinations.

35
00:03:24,952 --> 00:03:29,531
The new pipeline shapes records only for OpenTelemetry.

36
00:03:30,431 --> 00:03:34,263
What goes in the secret for the Authorization header?

37
00:03:38,663 --> 00:03:44,087
The header's whole value: Bearer, a space, and the token.

38
00:03:44,987 --> 00:03:49,534
Next, you see what smeltr does when a destination fails.
